// The tree JSX builds — in `core` for the reason the shipped file gives, and unchanged in shape.
//
// `core/jsx.wac`: *"It is here rather than in a package because the compiler emits the constructor
// calls: a tree built in one repository and a renderer in another must name one type or nothing
// composes, and no author chose the name to be able to fix it."* That argument does not depend on
// anything vision proposes, so this file is the shipped one with two changes and a note about a
// third that was not made.
//
// **`Fragment` stays a variant** rather than an element with an empty tag, for the reason the
// shipped comment gives and which is a good statement of what an enum buys: *"a renderer is told —
// `match` is exhaustive, and a walk written before fragments existed stops compiling with the arm it
// is missing named. An empty tag would have rendered as `<>` in every renderer that had not heard of
// them."* `@/packages/page/src/render.wac` is that renderer and it has all three arms because it had
// to.
//
// ## Not a union, and this is the case that decides it cleanly
//
// `../IDIOMS.md`'s test is whether the members stand alone and whether the family wants methods.
// `packages/json`'s README worked through the same question for `JsonValue` and kept the enum; this
// is the easier instance of it. A `Text` is not a string — it is *a string in the position of a
// child*, and `Node.Text(s)` is the spelling `spec/spec/jsx.md` requires precisely so that a string
// child says what it is. A member that means nothing outside the family is an enum's member.
//
// ## What did change
//
// **`Bytes` and `Slice` are not used here**, deliberately, and it is worth saying why since almost
// everything else in `vision/core` took them. A `Node` is built by the *compiler*, from source, and
// then walked once — there is no subrange of a document, nobody slices a child list, and the
// allocation is one per element either way. `../bench/slicecost.wac` measures the case where a slice
// pays and this is not it.
//
// **The attribute value is a `string` and should probably not be.** See below.

/** One attribute. */
export struct Attr {
  string name;
  string value;
}

/** A node. */
export enum Node {
  Element(string tag, Attr[] attrs, Node[] kids),
  Text(string text),
  Fragment(Node[] kids)
}

// ## What could not be written
//
// **An attribute whose value is not a string.** `<input size="24">` and `<div hidden>` and
// `<p data-n={n}>` are three different things in HTML and one type here, so a renderer cannot tell
// *the empty string* from *no value*, and `hidden=""` is what a boolean attribute becomes. The
// shipped `Attr` has the same two fields and the same hole; naming it needs `string?` at least, and
// a real answer is a small union — `Text(string) | Flag | Number(i64)` — which is a change to what
// the *compiler* constructs and so is not this file's to make.
//
// **A verbatim name is indistinguishable from an ordinary one by the time it gets here.**
// `@"for"` exists so a keyword can be an attribute, and it lands as `Attr("for", …)` — correct, and
// it means `Attr` accepts any string at all, including `"class name"`, which renders markup no
// parser reads back. The tag is a literal in the source, so this is checkable at the JSX and is
// checked nowhere. `@/packages/page`'s README has it as an open item on the rendering side; it is
// really this type's, because a validating renderer can only refuse at run time what the type let
// somebody write.
//
// **Whether a void element is a third variant.** `render` has to know that `<input>` takes no
// closing tag, so it carries a list of fourteen tag names — a renderer knowing something about HTML
// that the tree does not. `Void(string tag, Attr[] attrs)` would move it here and make
// `<input>{x}</input>` unconstructable, which is the version the compiler could enforce. Against it:
// `core` would then know HTML, and this type is deliberately about *markup* rather than about one
// vocabulary of it — `Element("my-widget", …)` is legal and should be.
